The important division is between **source ownership** and **entry
activation**. A root file selects a role. Importing a public declaration never
runs an imported module's entry.

### Who owns each piece?

- `config.moth` supplies optional project and builder settings. It never joins
  the module graph.
- `@entry.moth` owns one selected page's direct fragments, builder slots and
  runtime start. It publishes no API.
- `@package.moth` publishes a scoped support surface to eligible sibling
  branches and their descendants.
- `@mod.moth` publishes a normal module surface to its parent.
- Ordinary `.moth` files hold shared implementation inside their module. They
  stay private by default.
- `.mtf` files produce compile-time String content. They never read the entry's
  private bindings.

A directory can have any subset of the three root files. Each root keeps its
own declaration scope even when the files share one structural module. `$export`
belongs to the two facade roles and cannot expose entry work.

### Compilation is not activation

A build compiles the modules it needs. Only the chosen page's entry runs.

[codeblock, $code "text":
    build selects entries
      -> compile each module once
      -> bind public declarations
      -> activate the selected page entry
]

Checking a project, importing a package or reading a facade exposes
declarations. None of those steps start page work.

### Keep module dependencies deliberate

The example's `@components` support package is visible to the site root, the
`docs` branch and its child pages because of where it sits. It stays a scoped
package, not a global import. Another support package at the same level cannot
import it merely because the two sit side by side.

A dependency clause selects names, not a runnable file. Organise components
behind a facade when several pages need the same public surface. Keep private
markup helpers close to the package's implementation. Move a declaration into a
child module when that module has its own meaningful public API.

Do not use an old `+*.moth` root or invent page names such as `@functions.moth`.
Only `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` and `@entry.moth` establish root roles.

### Why the entry stays simple

Direct top-level `["[...]"]` fragments must fold at compilation. The compiler
keeps their source order and the builder assembles them into the final document,
so startup never builds static content.

A direct `["~[...]"]` fragment opts into runtime output. The page's entry runs
once when selected. The builder publishes runtime fragments only after a
successful start, so a failed start cannot publish half of that invocation.
Earlier static fragments remain.

Use `#=` when a folded template needs a name, a field or an importable value.
That binding stays data: declaring it emits no page.

### HTML belongs to the builder

The HTML builder owns directory routes, metadata destinations, resource output
and URL rendering. Only a direct entry-root `["[$in title:...]"]` or another
registered builder contribution reaches it. An ordinary nested `$in` targets
its immediate template receiver.

Resources stay structural Strings until the consuming output has a URL context.
A shared component can carry a stylesheet reference without guessing the URL for
each page. Reusing one resource origin across several pages does not duplicate
the file.

See @../../project-structure (Project structure), @../../packages (Packages) and
@../../constants (Constants) for the precise source and builder contracts.
